const sections = [];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Why visit Lake Mburo if there are no lions?', a: 'Lions were historically present in Lake Mburo but are no longer resident. The park is worth visiting for its unique combination of species not found in other Uganda parks (zebras, impalas, topis, elands), the boat safari on the lake, and the walking safari inside the park. It is also the closest national park to Kampala, making it ideal for a short break or as a stopover on the way to western Uganda.' },
  { q: 'Is Lake Mburo good for a day trip from Kampala?', a: 'Yes, though an overnight stay is strongly recommended to get the most from the park. The drive from Kampala takes approximately 3 hours. A same-day trip is possible but leaves limited time for a game drive and boat safari. One night allows a morning game drive, boat safari and either a walking safari or night drive.' },
  { q: 'Can I see the Big Five at Lake Mburo?', a: 'No. Lake Mburo does not have lions, leopards or rhinos within the park. It has elephants (occasionally) and buffaloes. For Big Five game viewing, Murchison Falls and Queen Elizabeth are the recommended Uganda parks. Lake Mburo is best appreciated for its unique species, accessible walking activities and relaxed atmosphere.' },
];
